Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.909 --> 00:00:06.670
We are going to use EDirect to access a local
installation of PubMed.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:15.190
I am Peter Cooper, and as I said, Jonathan
Kans is sort of mind behind EDirect and he

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:21.140
is with us and Ben Busby is here and we are
here to answer your questions.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:26.340
What we are going to do today is learn to
use EDirect to archive PubMed on local solid

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:27.340
state drives.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:32.210
Where going to see how we can access the data
used in some of the functions now available

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:38.090
on EDirect , and we will also just point out
to you that you can search for terms, although

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:39.960
I want demonstrate that today.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.250
We are going to show you the basics on how
to get records out of there.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:48.930
I will also point out new features of the
xtract program and scripts that will point

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:55.160
out that you can process the data that you
get from PubMed locally with xtract without

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:56.680
having to do any additional scripts.

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:59.680
That is the powerful thing about xtract.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:10.770
Just a reminder, if you don't know what entrez
direct is, but you might be able to get caught

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.660
up if you check it out.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:17.300
This is a commandline version basically of
the E-utilities which are the API for the

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.800
Entrez system.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:27.770
That is limiting on what EDirect can do because
it has processing scripts and an XML parser

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:34.810
called xtract that are probably the most powerful
aspects of these programs.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:40.610
The retrieval program outputs to XML that
can be piped to another EDirect program or

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:43.989
Linux utility.

00:01:43.989 --> 00:01:47.509
You need to have UNIX or Linux to run this.

00:01:47.509 --> 00:01:53.621
Today we are demonstrating things on a Mac
and the documentation is there and there's

00:01:53.621 --> 00:01:58.900
a good manual that has lots and lots of examples
and you can link to that to find out more

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:00.200
about EDirect.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:07.410
Here is a diagram that shows you the different
functions or different programs that are in

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:12.470
EDirect . Basically the idea is you can run
a search, then you can retrieve things from

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:17.710
the history server using an efetch program,
and you can process those output.

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:23.700
What we are going to do today is show you
how to use a script that will fetch things

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.390
from a local instance of PubMed.

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:29.720
Then you can use xtract to parse that XML.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:34.270
We did do a webinar on EDirect and that is
useful if you want to check that out.

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:41.620
There is a link on this slide and that is
in our webinars playlist on our YouTube channel.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:47.450
Many people are interested in text mining
PubMed and particular the abstracts.

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:53.870
If you take some human disease mesh term like
this one, heart disease[mesh], and do a search,

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:57.990
you get a lot of records and you would get
a lot more for other kinds of searches, but

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:07.430
this is about 630,000 abstracts, and you can
go to the send to menu in the Entrez web system

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:12.500
and select file and download records and can
use any Entrez database.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:18.650
In this case you could get the XML of the
PubMed, which contains the abstract, and download

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:19.650
this.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.150
This will probably not work.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:28.780
It is a too large of set to do this way, and
even our APIs and in this case I am showing

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:33.900
you esearch, but it is showing a proxy for
any kind of API or any kind of programm or

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:38.120
script using E-utilities, those are going
to be slow.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:40.940
Here is what the history server tells us.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:47.209
Their 630,000 records that have abstracts,
which we already knew from that web search,

00:03:47.209 --> 00:03:55.650
and I could write a command line with piping
that into efetch or fetch if I was using on

00:03:55.650 --> 00:04:02.270
the E-utilities, and I could try to download
this and what you will find is probably it

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:07.099
won't work, at least not in one fell swoop.

00:04:07.099 --> 00:04:09.599
It is to slow.

00:04:09.599 --> 00:04:16.910
Just a reminder, the network service that
APIs accessing is very busy and a shared resource,

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:21.880
and access speed and the volume that you can
get from that is limited.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:25.330
There are lots of ways around that.

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:31.600
Where going to talk about a way today that
will help a lot, I think.

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:33.810
You could get all of PubMed.

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:36.380
You cannot get it by going to the web.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:41.030
You could download this by FTP.

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:54.570
You can now download PubMed Medline and you
can get a full XML dump without a license

00:04:54.570 --> 00:05:01.840
and there's an annual baseline and you can
get daily update files.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:06.921
Once you download it, the problem is you are
faced with several problems and one of them

00:05:06.921 --> 00:05:12.570
is there is no provided means of random access.

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:18.300
The xtract can parse it but you're going to
have process the entire set in some way, and

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.970
that is a big deal.

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:24.010
There lots of people that want to do these
kinds of things and Ben pointed out to me

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:29.390
the other day that there are two sort of programs
ClusterDuck and PuRSSE.

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:40.610
These are basically pipelines to work off
of downloading things from PubMed . They would

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:50.050
benefit greatly from using the strategy that
we are going to talk about today.

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:54.980
This problem of the local instance of PubMed,
EDirect has a solution thanks to Jonathan,

00:05:54.980 --> 00:06:00.840
and there are some scripts that now come with
EDirect that will install and update PubMed.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:06.980
There is a script called archive-pubmed that
will install and update a local instance of

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:09.090
PubMed.

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:16.400
It uses our FTP site downloads the files from
there and you can use it to keep it updated.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:22.510
This particular solution uses the Apple file
system on a solid-state drive to store records

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:26.590
in sort of hierarchal indexed folder system.

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:34.000
It uses Apple file system because that allows
you to have millions of files and folders.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.200
Talking with Jonathan, this is portable potentially
to other file systems.

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:44.370
We don't have the real details on how to do
that, but it should be possible, and maybe

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:49.240
Jonathan has some things to say about that
at the end if there are questions about that.

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:55.790
There's also an index-pubmed script that does
similar things for words and phrases in the

00:06:55.790 --> 00:07:03.130
local installation that uses the same sort
of strategies of files and folders, and EDirect

00:07:03.130 --> 00:07:08.800
efetch, I should have a different name for
that there in the slide, can retrieve from

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:19.570
the local cache and it is 100 times faster
than the network service, probably an underestimate.

00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:28.270
This link at the bottom of the slide, that
will take you to the portion of EDirect documentation

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:32.160
that talks about using the local Cache.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:33.160
https://go.usa.gov/xQMY7

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:34.430
Here are just some examples.

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:38.040
We are not going to try to run the script
today during the webinar because it will take

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:42.020
a long time to make your installation.

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:47.729
Basically you're going to tell it archive-pubmed
and give it a path to some drive.

00:07:47.729 --> 00:07:54.600
This is the name of the hard drive that Jonathan
sent me and that is where the data is.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:08:01.830
And so this archive-pubmed script will retrieve
the XML from FTP site and make these hierarchal

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:08.020
sets of files and folders and it takes about
1 million folders containing 28 million comprised

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:09.169
PubMed records.

00:08:09.169 --> 00:08:20.170
And there is that path to the folder that
would contain this particular PMID 12345678.

00:08:20.170 --> 00:08:21.180
/Volumes/rose/Archive/12/23/56/78/12345678.xml.gz.

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:29.650
It uses pairs of numbers in the PMID to create
this tree , and then there are several pub

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:35.120
med records in that directory, but it breaks
the database up into manageable chunks.

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:42.309
It also has a feature of clean things up in
PubMed XML and you can run archive-pubmed

00:08:42.309 --> 00:08:47.660
on a weekly basis or something like that to
maintain the database and keep it current.

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:52.350
There's also a similar strategy that you can
use to index this data.

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:57.500
You can use this index-pubmed, giving it a
path to where it is going to go (/Volumes/rose/Postings/c/a/n/c/canc.trm)

00:08:57.500 --> 00:09:04.149
and it creates a hierarch set of terms and
files and folders, analogous to the way records

00:09:04.149 --> 00:09:08.860
are stored.

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:14.420
You can go into the Volumes directory, and
there's a postings directory that has folders,

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:23.769
for example, cancer is broken up into this
/c/a/n/c/canc, and then all the terms related

00:09:23.769 --> 00:09:25.529
to cancer in there.

00:09:25.529 --> 00:09:30.439
This service allows unrestricted wildcard
truncation so you can get all variants of

00:09:30.439 --> 00:09:38.000
therapy with an asterisk on the end, therap*,
you can do phrase queries, proximity searches.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:46.910
The program that runs it is called local-phrase-search
and it counts and it will pull out the PMIDs

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:49.319
for passing to archive-pubmed for getting
around.

00:09:49.319 --> 00:09:55.300
All of this is happening on this little hard
drive that is about as big as a cell phone.

00:09:55.300 --> 00:10:05.249
It holds all of PubMed plus index folders,
and it is attached to my Mac desktop in NCBI

00:10:05.249 --> 00:10:07.689
but it can be attached to a Mac laptop.

00:10:07.689 --> 00:10:16.759
This is a 500 gigabyte that is formatted with
the Apple file system and attached to a Macintosh

00:10:16.759 --> 00:10:19.230
computer at the moment.

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:24.149
And then here is some example command lines
and we will do demos in a minute that will

00:10:24.149 --> 00:10:27.279
use some of these kinds of things.

00:10:27.279 --> 00:10:30.569
Here's one where I can run esearch,
esearch -db pubmed -query "PNAS [JOUR]" -pub

00:10:30.569 --> 00:10:32.889
abstract | efetch -format uid | fetch-pubmed
-path /Volumes/rose/Archive &gt; pnas.xml

00:10:32.889 --> 00:10:38.180
and I'm trying to get articles from PNAS that
have abstracts.

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:44.779
